    place: "Israel under Ahab",
    description: "Called down fire in front of a nation, and the next day asked God to let him die.",
    contribution: "The prophet the Old Testament ends by promising to send again, and one of two men who appear with Jesus at the transfiguration.",
    biography:
      "Elijah arrives in the text without introduction or genealogy, simply announcing to King Ahab that there would be no rain except at his word. Then he disappears for three years, fed by ravens at a brook and then by a widow in Zarephath whose jar of flour did not run out.\n\nThe confrontation on Mount Carmel is the scene everyone remembers. Four hundred and fifty prophets of Baal on one side and Elijah alone on the other, two altars, and the agreement that the god who answered by fire was God. He let them go first and spent the day mocking them -- perhaps their god was busy, or travelling, or asleep. Then he soaked his own altar with water three times and prayed a short prayer, and the fire fell.\n\nWhat happens next is the reason the story is worth telling honestly. Jezebel sent word that she would have him killed within a day, and this same man ran for his life into the wilderness, sat under a broom tree and asked God to take his life, saying he was no better than his ancestors.\n\nGod\u2019s response was not a rebuke. An angel let him sleep, and woke him twice to eat. Then at Horeb came wind, earthquake and fire -- and God was in none of them, but in a still small voice, a low whisper, which asked him what he was doing there and then gave him work and told him he was not alone: seven thousand had not bowed to Baal.\n\nHe did not die. He was taken up in a whirlwind, and Elisha saw it.",
