    id: "bible-deborah",
    name: "Deborah",
    collection: "biblical",
    group: "judges-and-kings",
    wikipedia: "Deborah",
    timePeriod: "Judges 4-5",
    place: "The hill country of Ephraim",
    description: "A prophet and judge who held court under a palm tree, and went to war because the general would not go without her.",
    contribution: "The only woman among the judges, and the author of one of the oldest poems in the Bible.",
    biography:
      "Deborah judged Israel during the twenty years Jabin of Canaan oppressed them. She sat under a palm between Ramah and Bethel and the people came to her for judgement -- a woman functioning as prophet, judge and, when it came to it, war leader.\n\nShe summoned Barak and told him God's instruction: take ten thousand men to Mount Tabor, and Sisera with his nine hundred iron chariots would be delivered into his hand. Barak's answer was that he would go only if she went with him. She agreed, and told him that because of the way he was doing it the honour of the victory would go to a woman.\n\nIt did, and not to Deborah. Sisera's army was routed when the river Kishon flooded and his chariots bogged down. He fled on foot to what he thought was a friendly tent, and Jael drove a tent peg through his head while he slept.\n\nThe Song of Deborah in Judges 5 is the account in poetry, and linguists regard it as among the oldest Hebrew in the Bible. It names which tribes came and which stayed home, and it is unsparing about the ones who stayed. It ends with an unexpected turn: Sisera's mother at her window, wondering why her son's chariot is so long in coming, and her attendants inventing comforting explanations.",
    famousQuote: "Awake, awake, Deborah! Awake, awake, utter a song!",
