    place: "Jerusalem",
    description: "He saw the Lord in the temple, said he was ruined, and volunteered anyway for a mission he was told would fail.",
    contribution: "The prophet the New Testament quotes most, and the source of the Servant passages read at Christmas and Good Friday.",
    biography:
      "Isaiah prophesied in Jerusalem across the reigns of four kings, through the years when Assyria destroyed the northern kingdom and came within a siege of taking Jerusalem too.\n\nHis call is in chapter six. He saw the Lord seated on a throne, the temple filled with the train of his robe, and seraphim calling holy, holy, holy to one another. His response was not delight but dread -- woe is me, for I am undone, a man of unclean lips among a people of unclean lips. A coal from the altar touched his mouth. Then the question, and his answer: here am I, send me.\n\nWhat he was sent to say was that they would hear and not understand. He asked how long, and was told: until the cities lie waste.\n\nHe confronted kings. He told Ahaz to trust God rather than buy Assyrian protection, and gave the sign of a child called Immanuel. He stood with Hezekiah through Sennacherib's siege, when the Assyrian commander shouted taunts in Hebrew from the wall so the defenders could understand them.\n\nThe later chapters turn from judgement to comfort -- comfort my people -- and contain the four Servant Songs, culminating in the figure of chapter fifty-three who was despised and rejected, pierced for transgressions, and silent before his accusers. The New Testament writers reach for these passages more than any others.",
    famousQuote: "Here I am. Send me!",
    bibleVerse: {"text":"He was pierced for our transgressions. He was crushed for our iniquities. The punishment that brought our peace was on him.","reference":"Isaiah 53:5"},
