    wikipedia: "Jonah",
    timePeriod: "The book of Jonah",
    place: "Israel, the sea, and Nineveh",
    description: "The one prophet in the Bible who succeeded completely and was furious about it.",
    contribution: "A short book about a God whose mercy extends to people his own prophet wanted destroyed.",
    biography:
      "God told Jonah to go to Nineveh, the capital of Assyria -- the empire that would destroy his country -- and preach against it. Jonah went to Joppa and bought passage to Tarshish, roughly the opposite direction.\n\nA storm nearly broke the ship. The sailors, who were pagans, prayed and threw cargo overboard while Jonah slept below. When the lot fell on him he told them to throw him into the sea, and they tried hard to row back to land first before they did -- the pagans come out of this story better than the prophet does.\n\nA great fish swallowed him -- the text says fish, not whale -- and he was inside three days and prayed a psalm of thanksgiving from it before he was vomited onto dry land.\n\nHis sermon in Nineveh is eight words in Hebrew and shows no sign of effort. The entire city repented anyway, from the king down, and God relented.\n\nThis made Jonah angry enough to want to die, and his complaint is the theological centre of the book: he says he fled in the first place because he knew God was gracious and merciful, slow to anger and abounding in love, and would relent. He objected to the mercy.\n\nGod grew a plant to shade him and then killed it, and asked why he cared about a plant he did not make while feeling nothing for a hundred and twenty thousand people who could not tell their right hand from their left. The book ends on that question, unanswered.",
